  m_Name: NuclearReactorCoreData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Nuclear Reactor Core
  engineDescription: A Nuclear Reactor Core is the central region of a nuclear reactor
    where a controlled nuclear fission reaction takes place and releases thermal
    energy. It operates by maintaining a controlled chain reaction in which fissile
    fuel atoms split and release energy and neutrons. Its major components include
    fuel assemblies, fuel rods, control rods, moderator, coolant channels, neutron
    absorbers, structural supports, and monitoring systems. During operation, nuclear
    fission within the fuel generates heat, while the moderator, where used, slows
    neutrons to support the chain reaction. Control rods absorb neutrons and regulate
    reactor power, while the coolant continuously removes heat from the core and
    transfers it to a steam generator or other heat-removal system. Nuclear reactor
    cores are used in nuclear power plants, research reactors, and specialized propulsion
    systems. From an engineering perspective, reactor kinetics, neutron behavior,
    heat transfer, coolant flow, fuel integrity, reactivity control, radiation protection,
    and thermal-hydraulic safety are critical to reliable operation.
  engineCategory: Engineering
